<ul class="enc-list"><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhanga"><span class="enc-name">Dhanga</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dhanga was king of the Chandela dynasty of Bundelkhand from about 950 to 999.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhanya-manikya"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dhanya-manikya.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dhanya Manikya</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dhanya Manikya was king of Tripura from about 1490 to 1515 and one of the most powerful rulers of the Manikya dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhanyakataka"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dhanyakataka.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dhanyakataka</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dhanyakataka was an ancient city on the Krishna river in present-day Andhra Pradesh, India, at modern Dharanikota beside Amaravati.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhar-tichitt"><span class="enc-name">Dhar Tichitt</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dhar Tichitt is an escarpment in south-central Mauritania with extensive remains of stone-built settlements.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhar-walata"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dhar-walata.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dhar Walata</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dhar Walata is an escarpment in south-eastern Mauritania with archaeological remains of the Tichitt stone-settlement culture.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dharanidhar-naik"><span class="enc-name">Dharanidhar Naik</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dharanidhar Naik was a leader of the Bhuyan people of Keonjhar in Odisha, India, who led a revolt against the Raja of Keonjhar in 1891.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dharanikota"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dharanikota.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dharanikota</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dharanikota is a village on the Krishna river in the Guntur region of Andhra Pradesh, India, close to the great Buddhist stupa of Amaravati.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dharma"><span class="enc-name">Dharma</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Dharma is a central concept in Indian religions, denoting moral law, duty and righteous conduct.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dharmachakra"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dharmachakra.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dharmachakra</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">The Dharmachakra, or Wheel of the Law, is a Buddhist symbol representing the teaching of the Buddha, recalling his first sermon at Sarnath, known as setting the wheel…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dharmakshema"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dharmakshema.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dharmakshema</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dharmakshema was an Indian Buddhist monk and translator who worked in China under the Northern Liang ruler Juqu Mengxun.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dharmapala-pala-emperor"><span class="enc-name">Dharmapala (Pala emperor)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dharmapala was the second ruler of the Pala Empire of Bengal and Bihar, reigning from about 770 to 810.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dharmapala-of-kotte"><span class="enc-name">Dharmapala of Kotte</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dharmapala of Kotte was the last king of Kotte in Sri Lanka, placed on the throne as a child in 1551 under Portuguese protection.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dharmashastra"><span class="enc-name">Dharmashastra</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Dharmashastra is the body of Sanskrit treatises on dharma, the religious and legal duties of Hindus, of which the Laws of Manu is the best known.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dharmawangsa"><span class="enc-name">Dharmawangsa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dharmawangsa Teguh was king of the Medang kingdom in East Java from about 990 to 1016.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dharwad"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dharwad.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dharwad</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dharwad is a city in northern Karnataka, India, forming a twin city with Hubballi.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhatusena"><span class="enc-name">Dhatusena</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dhatusena was a king of the Anuradhapura Kingdom in Sri Lanka who reigned from about 455 to 473.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhaun-castle"><span class="enc-name">Dhaun Castle</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dhaun Castle, Schloss Dhaun, is a castle above the Simmerbach valley near Hochstetten-Dhaun in Rhineland-Palatinate, Germany, first recorded in the early 13th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhian-singh"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dhian-singh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dhian Singh</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dhian Singh was a Dogra noble who served as wazir (chief minister) of the Sikh Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhiban"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dhiban.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dhiban</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dhiban, biblical Dibon, is a town in Jordan east of the Dead Sea and north of the Arnon gorge.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhimiter-progoni"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dhimiter-progoni.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dhimitër Progoni</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dhimitër Progoni was the ruler of Arbanon, the first recorded Albanian principality, from about 1208 to 1215, succeeding his brother Gjin.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhimmi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dhimmi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dhimmi</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">Dhimmi is the status under Islamic law of protected non-Muslim communities, mainly Christians and Jews, who were permitted to practise their religion in return for…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhofar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dhofar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dhofar</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dhofar is the southern governorate of Oman, bordering Yemen, with its capital at Salalah.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dholavira"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dholavira.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dholavira</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dholavira is an archaeological site on Khadir Bet, an island in the Great Rann of Kutch in Gujarat, India, the remains of a city of the Indus Valley Civilisation.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhruva-dharavarsha"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dhruva-dharavarsha.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dhruva Dharavarsha</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dhruva Dharavarsha was a king of the Rashtrakuta dynasty of the Deccan who reigned from about 780 to 793.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhruvasena-ii"><span class="enc-name">Dhruvasena II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dhruvasena II, also called Baladitya, was a king of the Maitraka dynasty of Valabhi in Gujarat in the early 7th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhu-ghabat"><span class="enc-name">Dhu Ghabat</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Dhu Ghabat was the chief god of the oasis of Dedan, modern al-Ula in north-western Arabia, worshipped under the kingdoms of Dedan and Lihyan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhu-nuwas"><span class="enc-name">Dhu Nuwas</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dhu Nuwas was a Jewish king of Himyar in South Arabia in the early sixth century, remembered for his persecution of Christians at Najran.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhu-raydan"><span class="enc-name">Dhu Raydan</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Dhu Raydan, after Raydan, the royal palace at the Himyarite capital Zafar, was the name by which the Himyarite kingdom of south Arabia appears in its inscriptions.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhul-nunid-dynasty"><span class="enc-name">Dhul-Nunid dynasty</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Dhul-Nunid dynasty (Banu Dhi al-Nun) was a Berber family that ruled the Taifa of Toledo from the 1030s to 1085.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhulbahante"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dhulbahante.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dhulbahante</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Dhulbahante are a Somali clan of the Harti branch of the Darod, living mainly in the Sool region and parts of Sanaag and Togdheer.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhvanyaloka"><span class="enc-name">Dhvanyaloka</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Dhvanyaloka ('Light on Suggestion') is a Sanskrit treatise on poetics by the Kashmiri critic Anandavardhana, written in the 9th century under the Utpala dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/di-qing"><span class="enc-name">Di Qing</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Di Qing was a Chinese general of the Northern Song dynasty who led campaigns against the Western Xia and against the rebellion of Nong Zhigao in Guangxi.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/di-xin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/di-xin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Di Xin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Di Xin was the last king of the Shang dynasty of China.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/di-yi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/di-yi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Di Yi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Di Yi was the penultimate king of the Shang dynasty and father of Di Xin, the last Shang king.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/diaba-lompo"><span class="enc-name">Diaba Lompo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Diaba Lompo is the legendary founder of the Gulmance kingdom of Gulmu, centred on Fada N'Gourma in eastern Burkina Faso.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/diadochi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/diadochi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Diadochi</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Diadochi ('successors') were the generals and companions of Alexander the Great who fought over his empire after his death in 323 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/diaeus"><span class="enc-name">Diaeus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Diaeus was a general of the Achaean League who, with Critolaus, led it into its final war against Rome.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/diagoras-of-rhodes"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/diagoras-of-rhodes.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Diagoras of Rhodes</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Diagoras of Rhodes was a celebrated boxer from Ialysos, whose victory at the Olympic Games of 464 BCE was praised by Pindar in his seventh Olympian ode.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/diaguita"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/diaguita.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Diaguita</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Diaguita were a group of indigenous peoples speaking the Cacán language who lived in the valleys of north-western Argentina and north-central Chile.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/diakhao"><span class="enc-name">Diakhao</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Diakhao is a village in the Fatick Region of western Senegal that served as the royal capital of the Serer kingdom of Sine.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dial-diop"><span class="enc-name">Dial Diop</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dial Diop was a Lebou Muslim leader of the Cape Verde peninsula, in present-day Senegal, who led the revolt against the kingdom of Cayor in the 1790s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dialogue-of-a-man-and-his-ba"><span class="enc-name">Dialogue of a Man and His Ba</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Dialogue of a Man and His Ba is an Egyptian literary text of the Middle Kingdom, preserved on a single papyrus now in Berlin.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/diamantina"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/diamantina.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Diamantina</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Diamantina is a colonial town in Minas Gerais, Brazil, formerly the Arraial do Tijuco.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/diamond-sutra-of-dunhuang"><span class="enc-name">Diamond Sutra of Dunhuang</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Diamond Sutra of Dunhuang is a Chinese woodblock-printed copy of the Diamond Sutra, a Mahayana Buddhist scripture, dated to 868 in the Tang dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/diana-mythology"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/diana-mythology.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Diana (mythology)</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Diana was the Roman goddess of hunting, wild animals, the moon and childbirth, later identified with the Greek Artemis.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/diane-de-poitiers"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/diane-de-poitiers.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Diane de Poitiers</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Diane de Poitiers was a French noblewoman and the mistress of King Henry II, who was nearly twenty years her junior.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/diarmait-mac-mael-na-mbo"><span class="enc-name">Diarmait mac Máel na mBó</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Diarmait mac Máel na mBó was king of Leinster from about 1042 and one of the most powerful rulers in Ireland until his death in 1072.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/diarmait-mac-murchada"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/diarmait-mac-murchada.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Diarmait Mac Murchada</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Diarmait Mac Murchada was King of Leinster of the Uí Chennselaig dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/diawara"><span class="enc-name">Diawara</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Diawara are a Soninke clan of the western Sahel, found mainly in western Mali around Nioro and Kayes.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/diber"><span class="enc-name">Dibër</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dibër is a mountainous region of eastern Albania along the Black Drin, bordering North Macedonia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href